WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:11.260
There’s a mod for Red Dead Redemption II…
that lets you play as any animal in the game’s

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.260
ecosystem.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.710
And it is, quite literally, a transformative
experience.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:21.650
With this mod I tore through a town as a deer,
and I raced through the grass as a mountain

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.760
lion, and I soared through the skies as an
eagle…

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:30.140
And through it all, I gained a new understanding
of a world I thought I was already deeply

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.340
familiar with.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:39.680
I knew this virtual ecosystem was vibrant,
but by changing my perspective, I realized…

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:46.250
it’s alive.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:50.370
Some worlds feel like they exist long before
you press play.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:55.820
When a game pulls this off, the results can
be breathtaking — and sometimes even a little

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:56.820
uncanny.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:05.659
So let’s discover what makes a digital ecosystem
truly seem to have a life of its own…

00:01:05.659 --> 00:01:12.119
Even without mods that expand your perspective,
the world of Red Dead is obsessively detailed.

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:16.960
Snow realistically falls from branches as
you wander past, the eyes of owl’s glow

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.600
in the dark, depending on the ambient temperature,
there’s realistic growth and shrinkage of

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.660
your horse’s — okay we’re skipping that
— but it proves my point!

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:29.750
This game’s commitment to environmental
minutiae is absurd.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:35.270
Which is probably why playing as an animal
genuinely feels like a kind of… existential

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:36.270
voyage.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:41.479
I don’t want to over-exaggerate, but I mean
it when I say this game is so immersive, it’s

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:44.750
hard not to lose yourself within each new
perspective.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:51.860
Every animal made me see the ecosystem a different
way, and reevaluate my position within it.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:56.979
Inhabiting different roles in an ecosystem…
is even more essential to a game like Lost

00:01:56.979 --> 00:01:58.030
Ember.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:03.690
Developed by an indie studio, the entire experience
revolves around changing forms — choosing

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:08.700
the perfect time to transfer your consciousness
to a different link in the food chain.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.770
You play as a Soul Wanderer, a reincarnated
spirit that can periodically take over the

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:17.970
bodies of nearby lifeforms to navigate a hostile
environment.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.590
It’s a little tricky to get the hang of
at first, but the more you practice, the more

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:27.629
natural it becomes to constantly be changing
between different modes of existence.

00:02:27.629 --> 00:02:34.190
No two species feel the same: being a wombat
is a leisurely existence, while being a hummingbird

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:38.800
is frantic — fitting for an animal with
a heart that beats over a thousand times per

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:39.800
minute.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.750
And since every creature has their own terrain
they’re adept at navigating, you can never

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.510
settle into one form for too long.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:51.980
Like in Red Dead, the multiple perspectives
give you a greater understanding of larger

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:57.290
ecosystems, to the point where it’s impossible
to imagine the game without this mechanic.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:59.040
But here’s what’s weird.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.210
The shape-changing feature isn’t actually
supposed to be in Red Dead at all, and yet

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:07.380
like Lost Ember, it feels fundamental.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.540
For example, while playing this mod I realized
that every bird takes off slightly differently.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:17.459
It even seems that heavy species like condors
can’t take off directly from the ground,

00:03:17.459 --> 00:03:21.470
and need a realistically elevated position
to soar through the skies.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:26.970
But pretty much the only time you see birds
in this game is from a great distance on horseback,

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.470
so why include these intricacies?

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:34.310
This mod doesn’t add any animations — all
these elements were here in the base game

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:37.100
… just waiting to be uncovered.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:42.180
Each bird also has a distinctive flight pattern,
except for bats, which have their own, completely

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.640
unique flight system.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.569
I didn’t even know there were bats in this
game — I’ve never seen one before!

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:49.700
…I’ve beaten it twice!

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:55.459
…What compels a studio to focus this much
on tiny details — details that the vast

00:03:55.459 --> 00:03:58.400
majority of players are never going to uncover?

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:03.980
If any game can help us answer that question,
it has to be Dwarf Fortress, quite possibly

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:08.189
the most labyrinthine and complicated title
ever conceived.

00:04:08.189 --> 00:04:13.269
The product of almost twenty years of work
by a pair of brothers, every time you press

00:04:13.269 --> 00:04:19.100
start, the game generates a new world, calculating
rainfall and mineral distribution and temperature

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.870
and elevation and a hundred other things.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:27.700
You can watch thousands of years of simulated
history flicker by, a timeline of events generated

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:32.810
in the top corner chronicling wars and treaties
and betrayals and losses… and that’s just

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:34.250
the abridged version.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:40.169
The full history of my first game included
five-hundred, twenty-five thousand and eighty-six

00:04:40.169 --> 00:04:43.460
events — all before I started playing.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:48.240
Trying to actually beat Dwarf Fortress is
potentially even more complicated.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.789
But I think that bewildering intricacy is
the point to an extent.

00:04:52.789 --> 00:04:57.919
In the original text-based game, you manage
countless entities represented by letters,

00:04:57.919 --> 00:04:59.720
which can be overwhelming…

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:00.750
but also mesmerizing.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:06.160
I know I’ll never fully comprehend this
gargantuan bionetwork — but that just makes

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:12.990
it feel more like an actual world, the kind
of ecosystem you could spend a lifetime studying.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:17.800
Of course, you could also spend a lifetime
studying the smallest ecosystem.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:21.539
Ants Simulation is the pinnacle of specificity.

00:05:21.539 --> 00:05:26.319
Created by youtuber Pezzza, it’s incredible
how thoroughly this project replicates the

00:05:26.319 --> 00:05:28.569
movements of the real insects.

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:33.660
Every simulated worker leaves behind a chemical
path just like in nature to guide their nestmates

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.200
to sources of food.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:40.030
Two different colonies of ants will fight
over the same resources, the conflict realistically

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.490
breaking off into numerous skirmishes.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:47.320
Ant simulator doesn’t boast thousands of
years of lore like Dwarf Fortress, and yet

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:52.740
the interactions feel no less important, proving
that a micro-world can be just as impactful

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.370
as a macro one.

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:59.030
This is true in other digital ecosystems as
well: there’s a reason why players are so

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:04.280
excited to find ants in Skyrim, a title where
you regularly fight dragons.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:10.930
Sometimes, it’s the smallest details that
make an ecosystem feel alive.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:12.539
Take mud, for instance.

00:06:12.539 --> 00:06:14.479
Okay, hear me out on this.

00:06:14.479 --> 00:06:17.050
The mud in Red Dead is beautiful.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:23.220
The way people and animals and carts all leave
tracks through it adds so much to the environments.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:27.880
If you accidentally fall into the mud, it
gets all over your clothes — it seems so

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:28.900
physical.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:32.000
Which is why I was surprised to learn it’s
flat.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.449
And not just flat because it’s on a TV screen
— I mean flat compared to the rest of the

00:06:37.449 --> 00:06:39.150
game’s 3D rendering.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:44.009
People have gone into the files and discovered
this mud is just a 2D texture, with the deep

00:06:44.009 --> 00:06:47.479
footprints all a clever optical illusion.

00:06:47.479 --> 00:06:52.190
And that’s not a bad thing, you can’t
really render a virtual ecosystem on current

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:55.560
hardware without carefully allocating resources.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:01.000
But it is a noteworthy paradox that many of
the worlds that feel the most alive must constantly

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:02.680
cull things out.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:08.010
The beautiful, rippling grasses of games like
Tears of the Kingdom can only exist because

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:11.970
the world is lowering the quality of plants
that are further away.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:16.500
In fact, for most modern games, any part of
the terrain that isn’t within the player’s

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:20.310
immediate cone of vision is constantly being
rendered out.

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:25.310
And again, I want to make it clear that none
of these techniques are bad — without them,

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:28.790
most digital ecosystems couldn’t exist at
all.

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:35.470
So why is it that I feel slightly uneasy when
I learn that a game like Insomniac’s Spider-Man

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:39.870
contains thousands — tens of thousands — of
false environments?

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:44.919
The vast majority of interiors you can see
through windows in this game are illusions,

00:07:44.919 --> 00:07:49.669
completely flat images like Red Dead’s mud,
made to look full of depth through a clever

00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:50.930
trick of programing.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:56.580
It’s a conceit that makes total sense — New
York in this game is an urban ecosystem just

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:02.340
as detailed as any biological one; there’s
no way the developers could render every single

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:03.340
apartment.

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:04.630
I know that.

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:09.720
And yet there’s still something strange
to me about peering into a space and knowing

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.069
it’s not there.

00:08:13.069 --> 00:08:16.570
This strangeness isn’t unique to game environments.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:20.910
Movies have been using similar techniques
for decades, creating numerous fantastical

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:24.270
ecosystems through well-placed flat images.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:28.961
The traditional film method for creating imagined
environments was the matte painting, which

00:08:28.961 --> 00:08:33.080
was combined with live action footage in the
days before cgi.

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:37.729
The original Star Wars trilogy made use of
techniques like this a lot — with numerous

00:08:37.729 --> 00:08:42.410
scenes merging sets with paintings in ways
that are almost impossible to notice.

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:45.950
It really is something of a lost worldbuilding
art.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:50.959
It’s likely you’ve seen some landscapes
in movies you haven’t even realized were

00:08:50.959 --> 00:08:51.959
paintings.

00:08:51.959 --> 00:08:54.240
Even still, you might have felt something
was off.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.680
A slight, nagging sense of unreality.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:03.170
And of course, in some cases, it’s easier
to tell than others, with the very seams visible

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:05.880
if you look closely.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:09.640
The seams in virtual ecosystems typically
aren’t as obvious.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:12.730
But you can spot them too if you know what
to look for.

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:17.200
Any moment in a modern game that requires
you to stop and move through a tight space

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:20.990
is probably buying time to load in the next
area.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:26.350
This strategy is honestly pretty clever, and
shows how carefully streamlined virtual words

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:27.790
have to be.

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:33.870
Maybe that’s why when a game includes useless
details — like ants or overly-specific flight

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.790
animations, it feels so revelatory.

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:41.130
Like you turned around too fast and instead
of catching the seams, you caught the game

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:43.970
being alive in a way that it shouldn’t.

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:48.459
This is a huge part of why the ecosystem of
a game like Rain World seems to have a life

00:09:48.459 --> 00:09:49.579
of its own.

00:09:49.579 --> 00:09:54.690
In that title, creature interactions are simulated
even when you aren’t onscreen, which means

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:59.720
you’ll constantly stumble upon unfolding
dramas that have nothing to do with your journey.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:05.810
It feels like the world simply exists far
beyond the confines of the camera.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:11.220
If there’s a film equivalent to this kind
of environment, it’s probably the expansive,

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:15.360
360-degree physical set — which are becoming
increasingly rare.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.360
Though less practical than a static backdrop
or green screen, these types of sets have

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:24.459
total immersion quality I find extremely compelling.

00:10:24.459 --> 00:10:28.240
These are worlds that people actually built
and walked through.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:33.570
Like the Roman Forum from the film Cleopatra,
an absolutely monstrous construction that

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:35.370
bankrupted the studio.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:39.220
Its proportions were more than double that
of the actual Roman Forum.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:43.970
And since this film was a financial bomb,
it’s also something of a monument to the

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:49.540
hubristic end of old Hollywood, a historical
site in its own right — though not one that’s

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:51.010
survived to present day.

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:57.040
I also think of the Atoll set from Waterworld,
which was a quarter mile in circumference.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:01.970
Production of this movie reportedly swallowed
up every piece of steel in Hawaii, requiring

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.060
more to be flown in from California.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:07.960
This film was also a financial bomb.

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:13.210
You might question what such anecdotes have
to do with video game ecosystems, but I think

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:17.700
there’s a real commonality across these
obsessive attempts at crafting convincing

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.480
fictional environments.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:24.600
Perhaps it’s a natural creative inclination
to chase the dream of an imagined world without

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:25.600
boundary.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:30.820
It can also, clearly, be disastrous — and
even dangerous.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:34.820
At what point does a fictional world stop
being fictional?

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:38.020
This is a question essential to the production
of “Dau.”

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:43.320
What began as a single movie turned into a
two-year long experiment where over a thousand

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:48.519
actors and extras lived in a closed-off replica
of Cold-War era Russia.

00:11:48.519 --> 00:11:54.139
Whether on or off camera, people on set had
to remain fully in character, working actual

00:11:54.139 --> 00:11:56.769
jobs for period-appropriate currency.

00:11:56.769 --> 00:12:02.810
This Truman Show-like simulation was 12,000
square miles, the largest set ever built in

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:03.810
Europe.

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:08.329
And the institutional corruption the movie
grapples with reportedly spilled over into

00:12:08.329 --> 00:12:12.490
life on the set, with allegations of mistreatment
surrounding the production.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:17.310
And despite all these efforts to create authentic
setting — the movie isn’t even supposed

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:19.570
to be very good!

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:24.110
(Crowd Noises)
Did you know that there’s over a thousand

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:26.480
unique characters in Red Dead?

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:32.130
The vast majority exist in the background,
full-time extras in a digital set, their sole

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:36.290
purpose to make the environments you walk
through more lifelike.

00:12:36.290 --> 00:12:40.389
Yet if you decide to follow one throughout
their day, you’ll find most of these digital

00:12:40.389 --> 00:12:43.270
people have a full daily routine.

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:45.690
They wake up each morning and go to work.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:48.000
Some toil away for long hours.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:50.100
Others slack off when they can.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.149
When night falls, people amble home at different
speeds.

00:12:54.149 --> 00:12:56.460
Some have families to get back to.

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:59.190
Others seem… achingly lonely.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:04.630
Early in this video, I said that worlds that
seem like they exist before you press play

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:06.050
can feel uncanny.

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:11.640
Red Dead’s world, with its almost unhinged
devotion to realism, can certainly fall into

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:13.680
that category.

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:19.020
Left unchecked, the pursuit of a living fictional
world can be a kind of madness.

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:23.800
You can see this quite plainly across the
multi decade career of game designer Peter

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:24.800
Molyneux.

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:30.519
A founding influence in the so-called ‘God-Games’
genre, his projects frequently give you command

00:13:30.519 --> 00:13:35.960
of not just ecosystems, but entire worlds,
and are usually ambitious to the point of

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:40.690
collapsing under their own weight — the
Cleopatras and Waterworlds of gaming.

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:45.010
There’s a reason why not that many other
studios have even attempted to make games

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:50.370
like this — trying to make worlds at this
level of complexity can often lead to setbacks

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:52.250
and broken promises.

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:57.639
That said, a part of me sympathizes with how
Molyneux’s desire to create outpaces what

00:13:57.639 --> 00:13:59.430
he can actually produce.

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:04.320
The wildly anticipated Fable famously claimed
players could plant an acorn and watch it

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:08.990
grow into a tree over the course of the game,
a feature that had to be cut in the final

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:11.230
version.

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:16.100
The game Yonder: The Cloud Catcher Chronicles
was not produced by Molyenux.

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:21.260
Yet as writer Matt Paprocki points out in
his review of the title… in that game, you

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:24.110
actually can watch acorns grow into trees.

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:29.350
Which is impressive, because Yonder is a small
indie project — but one with a strong sense

00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:31.770
of the kind of world it wants to build.

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:37.560
The map is tiny, the creature variety limited
— yet Yonder’s ecosystem feels more fully

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.130
realized than many big-budget titles.

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:45.930
I think it’s an intriguing contrast to Dau-style
megaprojects, suggesting that not all worlds

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:49.750
need a thousand layers of detail to feel alive.

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:53.320
Do Red Dead’s intricacies make the game
better?

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:58.690
I mean, don’t get me wrong, they certainly
do part of the time — these might be the

00:14:58.690 --> 00:15:02.670
most stunning environments I’ve ever had
the pleasure of exploring.

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:08.310
But on the other hand, sometimes the excess
feels like… well, excess.

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:13.350
I think one detail in particular sums up this
dichotomy the best, and it’s probably one

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:14.440
you’ve never noticed.

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:20.380
The stars in Red Dead shine ever so slightly
more brightly in the wilderness than they

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:22.630
do in urban areas.

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:24.490
Which is accurate, but also…

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:27.850
what a detail to include!

00:15:27.850 --> 00:15:34.209
On the one hand it seems crazy to bother with
adding such a subtle real-world imperfection.

00:15:34.209 --> 00:15:37.720
But on the other hand, I think it’s kind
of perfect.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:42.940
Because Red Dead Redemption II isn’t just
a game about exploring a breathtaking world;

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:45.360
it’s also about the end of one.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:50.420
Rapid industrialization changed the very landscape
of the United States.

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:56.230
Expanding cities and railways replaced forests
and fields, destroying and displacing indigenous

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:57.339
communities.

00:15:57.339 --> 00:16:01.440
All these themes are present in the game’s
lengthy story, but they’re also present

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:03.319
in the land itself.

00:16:03.319 --> 00:16:08.029
Even when you’re not in a city, there’s
a transient quality to the game’s ecosystems,

00:16:08.029 --> 00:16:11.290
a sense that none of this will remain as it
is.

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:14.170
Not even the stars.

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:19.040
I felt this dimming of a natural era most
strongly when inhabiting the perspective of

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:20.139
animals.

00:16:20.139 --> 00:16:25.480
Which is again, really weird, because none
of that is meant to be in the game.

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:30.790
But trying to steer a buffalo through an urban
area is so visually and mechanically powerful

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:36.500
— it instills a feeling in your animal bones
that this is a world that no longer belongs

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:37.500
to you.

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:42.930
Maybe it’s silly to read this much into
a fan-made add-on, but I think it’s a testament

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:48.300
to how thorough this world is that it can
serve as a canvas for experiences the developers

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:50.570
never intended.

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:55.240
Having said all this, I do think there needs
to be some reckoning with the fact that these

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:57.720
ecosystems don’t always work perfectly.

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:02.709
I’ve recorded a lot of game footage for
this channel, and one thing I always edit

00:17:02.709 --> 00:17:08.650
out are glitches — moments where creatures
freak out or just behave in an unusual manner.

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:13.329
Even the most impressive virtual biospheres
have these sorts of hiccups.

00:17:13.329 --> 00:17:18.180
When it comes to immersion, glitches are almost
universally regarded as a negative.

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:23.640
But in a strange way, I think they can sometimes
make a world feel more realistic.

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:28.630
Nature doesn’t always act rationally, just
look at the Comedy Wildlife Photography Awards

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:34.760
— a contest where the top nature photographers
share photos of animals, essentially, glitching

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:35.760
out.

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:40.081
Animals aren’t always perfect at being animals
in real life, so it’s kind of fitting that

00:17:40.081 --> 00:17:42.570
the same is true in the virtual realm.

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:47.740
For example, I had a lot of trouble recording
footage of bears in Red Dead for this video,

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:51.880
because some problem with their code meant
they just would not do what I wanted.

00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:57.250
But I think that’s kind of appropriate — bears
don’t like doing what people tell them to,

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:58.630
they’re bears!

00:17:58.630 --> 00:18:03.820
I’m not sure any ecosystem embodies the
playful side of nature better than the one

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:06.260
in Alba: A Wildlife Adventure.

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:11.870
The final title I want to discuss today, Alba
is something of the spiritual and mechanical

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:13.710
opposite of a game like Red Dead.

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:18.270
Its world is bright and stylized instead of
grim and realistic.

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:22.730
Instead of playing as an outlaw you play as
a kid trying to document all the animals on

00:18:22.730 --> 00:18:23.730
an island.

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:28.500
And the map itself is really tiny — a carefully
curated slice of life.

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:33.880
You even have a little book full of all the
wildlife you can find, it’s… really charming.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:38.809
Which is why it’s a surprise to learn that
this world, too, is coming to an end.

00:18:38.809 --> 00:18:43.270
You find out early in the game that all these
environments will be replaced to make room

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:45.370
for a hotel development.

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:49.790
And as you might imagine, this world’s fate
isn’t as unalterable as the fate of the

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:51.420
United States in Red Dead.

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:53.559
I mean, it’s a kids’ game.

00:18:53.559 --> 00:18:57.380
But it’s telling that the creators felt
the need to include this element.

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:03.710
I don’t really think you can craft an ecosystem-centric
story without themes of ecological destruction

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:04.990
seeping in.

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:10.909
Even Lost Ember, which takes place in a fantasy
realm radically divergent from our own, subtly

00:19:10.909 --> 00:19:16.919
explores the tension between nature and humanity
through its story of a fallen civilization.

00:19:16.919 --> 00:19:21.590
These are ideas you can’t really escape,
even if they’re something only felt in the

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:23.520
background.

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:28.250
But in games with living ecosystems, the backgrounds
become the foregrounds.

00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:32.100
I know this is an aspect of Red Dead that
frustrated some people.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:37.301
I mean — the game is advertised as this
action-centric cowboy adventure — and it

00:19:37.301 --> 00:19:39.440
is that, for some of the time.

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:44.760
But at least 60 percent of this game is just
riding your horse through natural vistas.

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:49.750
On my first play through, that seemed like
a weird allocation of time, but now, I’m

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:50.890
not so sure.

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:57.430
In environment-focused games, it is the backdrop,
the minutia, and all those little, pointless

00:19:57.430 --> 00:19:59.990
details… that become the whole point.

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:04.890
My experiences with these worlds have stayed
with me, they’re something I carry around

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:07.039
in the jumble of my thoughts.

00:20:07.039 --> 00:20:11.169
Long after I’ve pressed the power button
and turned off these ecosystems for good,

00:20:11.169 --> 00:20:13.760
they’ve continued to have a life of their
own.

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:15.700
These worlds linger.

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:18.230
They’re alive.

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:20.679
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:20:20.679 --> 00:20:25.530
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:30.350
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:51.700
See you in the next video.

